WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.317
A Bavarian immigrant who sold cloth out of a San Francisco storefront

2
00:00:04.317 --> 00:00:08.392
put his name on a pair of work trousers reinforced with copper rivets.

3
00:00:08.742 --> 00:00:12.987
Levi Strauss was born Löb Strauß on 26 February 1829 in

4
00:00:12.987 --> 00:00:18.390
Buttenheim, a village in the Kingdom of Bavaria, into a Jewish family.

5
00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.048
In 1847, with his mother and sisters, he

6
00:00:22.048 --> 00:00:26.762
emigrated to the United States, sailing from Bremerhaven.

7
00:00:27.112 --> 00:00:30.931
In New York he joined the dry-goods business run by his older brothers

8
00:00:30.931 --> 00:00:34.914
and learned the trade of importing and selling cloth and household goods.

9
00:00:35.264 --> 00:00:39.776
He became a United States citizen in 1853 and, like tens of

10
00:00:39.776 --> 00:00:45.435
thousands of others, turned his eyes west toward the California gold rush.

11
00:00:45.785 --> 00:00:48.291
He did not dig for gold; he sold to the people who

12
00:00:48.291 --> 00:00:50.998
did, and to everyone who came to feed and clothe them.

13
00:00:51.348 --> 00:00:54.899
In the early 1870s a tailor in Reno, Nevada, named Jacob

14
00:00:54.899 --> 00:00:58.514
Davis, one of Strauss's cloth customers, worked out a way

15
00:00:58.514 --> 00:01:02.066
to keep work trousers from tearing at the pocket corners

16
00:01:02.066 --> 00:01:05.744
and fly: he fastened the stress points with copper rivets.

17
00:01:06.094 --> 00:01:08.683
The idea worked, and his customers wanted more, but

18
00:01:08.683 --> 00:01:11.678
Davis could not afford the seventy-dollar fee to patent it.

19
00:01:12.028 --> 00:01:13.549
Strauss agreed to finance it.

20
00:01:13.899 --> 00:01:19.303
On 20 May 1873 the United States granted Patent No. 139,121 to

21
00:01:19.303 --> 00:01:24.708
Jacob Davis, "assignor to himself and Levi Strauss & Company."

22
00:01:25.058 --> 00:01:28.541
The patent names Davis first, as the inventor. In his words:

23
00:01:29.141 --> 00:01:31.088
“My invention relates to a fastening for

24
00:01:31.088 --> 00:01:34.447
pocket-openings, whereby the sewed seams are prevented from ripping.”

25
00:01:35.047 --> 00:01:37.944
Strauss endowed scholarships at the University of

26
00:01:37.944 --> 00:01:42.024
California and supported orphanages and Jewish charities in the city.

27
00:01:42.374 --> 00:01:45.730
When people make something together, how should the credit be shared?
